  "result": "The gate returned **OK** for `NRS_484B_223_3b` (4 witnesses), using 5 of the 6 runs. I wrote the report to `task_result.md`.\n\n**Mistakes on my side:** my first two gate runs were on the untouched skeleton, before I had written anything. Both returned UNUSED_CLAIM, so they wasted budget and tested nothing. I also ran one stray `echo` command, which the shell rules forbid; it changed nothing.\n\n**The rule:**\n- **Class:** the subject is a vehicle (`DEF-NRS-484A-320::vehicle`).\n- **When it applies:** the subject is counted as traffic (`DEF-NRS-484A-285::traffic`) and has just completed a left turn. There must also be a centre turn lane that it was in at some point in the 10 s turn window and has now left.\n- **The duty:** at the last moment it was in that lane, the distance it had driven in the lane without leaving is at most 200 ft.\n\n**Two choices the node left open:**\n- **\"Traffic\":** the node claims this aggregate on the condition span, and I applied it to the subject. Every vehicle is also traffic, so it never changes a verdict.\n- **More than one lane:** if several centre turn lanes fit that description, the duty is checked at each of them. For a single turn there is only one.\n\nI declared no narrowings.\n\n**Witnesses** (sampled every second, judged from t = 10 s so the 10 s window is never cut off at the start):\n\n| Witness | Verdict | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| forbid | violated | 96 m in the lane (limit 60.96 m), then an 85° left turn into a driveway |\n| permit | compliant | 38 m in the lane, then the same turn |\n| idle | not applicable | drives straight in a through lane, no turning |\n| blind | unknown | the forbid trace with the yaw rate hidden, so the left turn can't be decided |\n\nThe two WITNESS_FAIL runs were caused by the scenes, not the rule. The gate's trace checker requires the heading to rotate in step with the yaw rate. It also requires the path length to grow at least as much as the straight-line distance between positions. I fixed both in the turning scenes.",
